/-!
# Displacement in the genuine triple cover of M₂₂

Under the source conditions `IsTripleM22 L` and F*(G) = L, every faithful
irreducible binary G-module has degree divisible by six. Noncentral cubic
subgroups move four times one sixth of the degree, and every ambient
involution moves at least one third of the module. Consequently an involution
moving at most four dimensions forces degree at most twelve, independently
of the minimum-degree and uniqueness argument.

In dimension twelve, all odd-prime-order ambient elements move at least eight
dimensions, and an actual noncentral cubic element attains eight. The five
bound uses a constructed Frobenius subgroup of order 55; eleven moves at least
ten; seven moves all twelve via the proved self-duality of restriction.
The resulting module input is connected to the existing H.12 local geometry,
including its literal unitary coordinate compatibility.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith H.12.1, printed pp.393–394 (PDF pp.404–405), combined
1 March 2004 manuscript. The parent Section10To12 combines the degree upper
bound with recognition for clause (3) and assembles beta = 8 from the odd-prime
bounds. The small-displacement hypothesis concerns U, as in the source proof.
-/
